From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Norman Herms <dockseed@proton.me>
Date: Mon, 5 Oct 2026 19:21:56 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] PM / devfreq: Add the Allwinner A733 DSU driver

The DynamIQ Shared Unit (DSU) of the A733 clocks the L3 cache and the
snoop filter of all eight cores. Boot firmware leaves it at one rate,
whatever the cores run at, and nothing in Linux changes it.

Add a devfreq driver that ties the DSU clock to the cores. The DSU runs
at the lowest operating point at or above three quarters of the fastest
core's rate (user manual, 5.1.4.1). The DSU shares its supply with the
Cortex-A55 cluster, so it only takes points whose voltage the A55
already holds; where none of them reaches three quarters, it takes the
highest of them. While the A76 runs at 1488 MHz or more, the DSU takes
at least the highest point at its lowest voltage. The A76 rate of the
floor is the vendor's value. The A55 policy is the one whose CPU has
the DSU's supply as cpu-supply. The DSU holds its own vote on that
regulator, so the rail stays up until the DSU has come down; on unbind,
the DSU goes to its lowest point before that vote is dropped. An
optional AXI divider runs at DSU/1 at the low points and at DSU/2
above, the two ratios of 5.1.4.1.

The driver has a governor of its own, as tegra30-devfreq has: the
passive governor takes the highest rate of the CPU policies, and
required-opps link fixed points; neither can cap the DSU by the voltage
of the A55 cluster. The speed bin selects the voltage column as in the
CPU tables, with a worst-case column for an unknown die.

Not covered: a cluster that goes offline, whose last rate the policy
keeps using.

Tested on Radxa Cubie A7S: booted on two boards; the DSU follows the CPU
policies at the point the rule gives for every tested pair of A55 and
A76 rates, the AXI divider is /1 at the lowest point and /2 from the
next tested point on, DCDC5 stays at the voltage of the A55 in every
pair, a forced DSU minimum raises DCDC5 and not DCDC3, and rmmod leaves
the DSU at its lowest point before the A55 lets DCDC5 down, after which
the module loads again.

Assisted-by: LLM
---
